<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isabella I of Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1451">1451</a>–<a href="/years/1504">1504</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trastamara">House of Trastámara</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isabella I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was Queen of Castile who, with her husband Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, united the Spanish crowns. In 1492 they completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s first voyage. Their reign laid the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile.jpg" alt="Isabella I of Castile" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IsabellaofCastile03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Isabella was born on 22 April 1451 at Madrigal de las Altas Torres, the daughter of King John II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and his second wife, Isabella of Portugal. When her father died in 1454, her half-brother became King Henry IV, and she and her brother Alfonso were left in his care. On 19 October 1469 she married Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> in secret at Valladolid. Because they were second cousins they needed a papal dispensation, and the bull produced was supposedly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a>'s, though he had died in 1464.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Henry IV denounced the marriage and in October 1470 declared his daughter Joanna his true heir. When he died on 11 December 1474, Isabella took refuge in the Alcázar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a> and was proclaimed Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León the next day. Supporters of Joanna planned to marry her to King Afonso V of Portugal. The war ended in a Castilian victory on land and a Portuguese victory at sea, and by the treaties of Alcáçovas, signed on 4 September 1479, Portugal gave up the Castilian throne and kept most of the disputed Atlantic territories, apart from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>.</p><p>Isabella tackled the disorder left by her brother's reign and in 1476 created a police force, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-hermandad">Santa Hermandad</a>. With Ferdinand she completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>, ordered the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the Alhambra Decree, began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a> and financed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>'s 1492 voyage. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada-war">Granada War</a> began in 1482, and it took ten years to conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. After a Muslim uprising in 1499, the monarchs broke the Treaty of Granada in 1502 and ordered Muslims to convert or leave.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Isabella withdrew from government on 14 September 1504 and died on 26 November at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo">Medina del Campo</a>. She is buried in the Capilla Real in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> beside Ferdinand. Of her seven children five survived to adulthood, among them Joanna, Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>. A cause for her beatification was opened in 1958 and she was named a Servant of God in 1974, but John Paul II suspended the cause in 1991 because of the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isabella I of Castile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The marriage of John’s son Ferdinand II to <b>Isabella of Castile</b> in 1469 led to a dynastic union with the Crown of Castile from 1479, and from 1516 both crowns passed to the Habsburgs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The marriage of <b>Isabella I of Castile</b> and Ferdinand II of Aragon in 1469 had joined their crowns in the person of the monarch, while each kingdom kept its own laws and institutions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The marriage of Ferdinand II of Aragon and <b>Isabella I of Castile</b> in 1469 then joined the Crowns of Aragon and Castile under the Catholic Monarchs, the foundation of the Spanish monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It was built very quickly after two events: the dynastic union of the crowns of Castile and Aragon under <b>Isabella I of Castile</b> and Ferdinand II of Aragon, complete from 1479, and the conclusion of the Reconquista with the fall of Granada in January 1492.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The port city of Almería, part of the Nasrid emirate of Granada, submitted to the sovereignty of the Catholic Monarchs, Ferdinand II of Aragon and <b>Isabella I of Castile</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-22">22 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Monasterio de Nuestra Señora de Gracia, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Palacio Testamentario, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.09,-5&amp;year=1485" aria-label="Monasterio de Nuestra Señora de Gracia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4428 698 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Isabella I of Castile"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4878 968h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4878 968h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.09,-5&amp;year=1485">What was here in 1485? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
